here is not just that this person understands you. It is that the two of you can keep learning how to understand each other more deeply.","policy":"Governed by synastry-policy. Descriptive, not deterministic; intensity is never treated as obligation, safety concerns override astrology.","note":"Imported from project-authored synastry matrix material. Source file was not copied into the repository.","status":"DRAFT","authoringStatus":"locked","weight":3}}'::jsonb, 'synastry_aspect', 'Seeded from authored synastry KB. summaryShort is preserved as NULL when absent or null.', now()),
  ('A-moon_B-moon_conjunction', 'synastry', 'in_depth', 'LIVE', 'synastry-aspect', null::date, '{"kind":"interaspect","planetA":"moon","planetB":"moon","aspect":"conjunction"}'::jsonb, ARRAY['A-moon_B-moon_conjunction', 'synastry-moon-conjunction-moon', 'relationship-moon-conjunction-moon']::text[], '{"contentType":"synastry-kb-seed","source":"packages/astro-knowledge","sourcePath":"packages/astro-knowledge/data/synastry/aspects/A-moon_B-moon_conjunction.json","collection":"synastry/aspects","kbId":"A-moon_B-moon_conjunction","importedStatus":"REVIEWED"}'::jsonb, 'synastry-kb-v1', null, 'Moon Conjunction Moon', null, 'You may feel comfortable with this person faster than you expected. Being around them can feel strangely familiar, as if they already understand parts of you that most people take longer to notice. There is often a sense that you do not have to work so hard to explain how you are feeling because they naturally seem to meet you where you are.

When this works, it can feel deeply reassuring. This person may have a way of responding that makes you feel understood rather than managed. You may notice yourself relaxing around them because there is less pressure to hide what you are feeling. Even on days when you are struggling, overwhelmed, or not quite yourself, you can feel accepted as you are. There is a quiet relief in not having to pretend everything is fine just to stay connected.

There can be a feeling of being emotionally understood without having to work for it. The things that make you feel cared for may be similar, so it becomes easier to look after each other in the ways that actually land. You may find yourselves creating a shared sense of comfort almost automatically.

The growth here is learning that understanding each other is not the same thing as helping each other grow. Because your emotional instincts are so similar, you may reinforce each other''s habits without questioning them. At times that is healing. At other times it can keep both of you stuck in the same mood, the same fear, or the same way of coping. This is one of the harder bonds to walk away from, because it can keep two people together long after the rest of the relationship has stopped working, simply because the comfort feels like home. The challenge is knowing when to offer comfort and when to gently help each other move forward.

This connection works best when safety becomes a place to recover, not a place to hide. Sometimes love is settling in together. Sometimes love is helping each other come back up.', '{"sections":[{"heading":"Deep meaning","body":"You may feel comfortable with this person faster than you expected. Being around them can feel strangely familiar, as if they already understand parts of you that most people take longer to notice. There is often a sense that you do not have to work so hard to explain how you are feeling because they naturally seem to meet you where you are.\n\nWhen this works, it can feel deeply reassuring. This person may have a way of responding that makes you feel understood rather than managed. You may notice yourself relaxing around them because there is less pressure to hide what you are feeling. Even on days when you are struggling, overwhelmed, or not quite yourself, you can feel accepted as you are. There is a quiet relief in not having to pretend everything is fine just to stay connected.\n\nThere can be a feeling of being emotionally understood without having to work for it. The things that make you feel cared for may be similar, so it becomes easier to look after each other in the ways that actually land. You may find yourselves creating a shared sense of comfort almost automatically.\n\nThe growth here is learning that understanding each other is not the same thing as helping each other grow. Because your emotional instincts are so similar, you may reinforce each other''s habits without questioning them. At times that is healing. At other times it can keep both of you stuck in the same mood, the same fear, or the same way of coping. This is one of the harder bonds to walk away from, because it can keep two people together long after the rest of the relationship has stopped working, simply because the comfort feels like home. The challenge is knowing when to offer comfort and when to gently help each other move forward.\n\nThis connection works best when safety becomes a place to recover, not a place to hide. Sometimes love is settling in together. Sometimes love is helping each other come back up."},{"heading":"Tension","body":"The growth here is learning that understanding each other is not the same thing as helping each other grow. Because your emotional instincts are so similar, you may reinforce each other''s habits without questioning them. At times that is healing. At other times it can keep both of you stuck in the same mood, the same fear, or the same way of coping. This is one of the harder bonds to walk away from, because it can keep two people together long after the rest of the relationship has stopped working, simply because the comfort feels like home. The challenge is knowing when to offer comfort and when to gently help each other move forward."},{"heading":"Advice","body":"This connection works best when safety becomes a place to recover, not a place to hide. Sometimes love is settling in together. Sometimes love is helping each other come back up."}],"synastryKb":{"id":"A-moon_B-moon_conjunction","kind":"interaspect","plainTranslation":"Your emotional natures mirror each other. You react the same way, need the same comfort, and feel instantly at home together. Failure mode: shared blind spots - if you both avoid conflict or both spiral, no one holds the other steady. Treasure the resonance and watch the patterns you''ll both reinforce.","summaryDeep":"You may feel comfortable with this person faster than you expected. Being around them can feel strangely familiar, as if they already understand parts of you that most people take longer to notice. There is often a sense that you do not have to work so hard to explain how you are feeling because they naturally seem to meet you where you are.\n\nWhen this works, it can feel deeply reassuring. This person may have a way of responding that makes you feel understood rather than managed. You may notice yourself relaxing around them because there is less pressure to hide what you are feeling. Even on days when you are struggling, overwhelmed, or not quite yourself, you can feel accepted as you are. There is a quiet relief in not having to pretend everything is fine just to stay connected.\n\nThere can be a feeling of being emotionally understood without having to work for it. The things that make you feel cared for may be similar, so it becomes easier to look after each other in the ways that actually land. You may find yourselves creating a shared sense of comfort almost automatically.\n\nThe growth here is learning that understanding each other is not the same thing as helping each other grow. Because your emotional instincts are so similar, you may reinforce each other''s habits without questioning them. At times that is healing. At other times it can keep both of you stuck in the same mood, the same fear, or the same way of coping. This is one of the harder bonds to walk away from, because it can keep two people together long after the rest of the relationship has stopped working, simply because the comfort feels like home. The challenge is knowing when to offer comfort and when to gently help each other move forward.\n\nThis connection works best when safety becomes a place to recover, not a place to hide. Sometimes love is settling in together. Sometimes love is helping each other come back up.","tension":"The growth here is learning that understanding each other is not the same thing as helping each other grow. Because your emotional instincts are so similar, you may reinforce each other''s habits without questioning them. At times that is healing. At other times it can keep both of you stuck in the same mood, the same fear, or the same way of coping. This is one of the harder bonds to walk away from, because it can keep two people together long after the rest of the relationship has stopped working, simply because the comfort feels like home. The challenge is knowing when to offer comfort and when to gently help each other move forward.","advice":"This connection works best when safety becomes a place to recover, not a place to hide. Sometimes love is settling in together. Sometimes love is helping each other come back up.","policy":"Governed by synastry-policy. Descriptive, not deterministic; intensity is never treated as obligation, safety concerns override astrology.","note":"Imported from approved project marquee synastry aspect material. Source file was not copied into the repository.","status":"REVIEWED","authoringStatus":"locked","weight":2}}'::jsonb, 'synastry_aspect', 'Seeded from authored synastry KB. summaryShort is preserved as NULL when absent or null.', now()),
  ('A-moon_B-moon_opposition', 'synastry', 'in_depth', 'LIVE', 'synastry-aspect', null::date, '{"kind":"interaspect","planetA":"moon","planetB":"moon","aspect":"opposition"}'::jsonb, ARRAY['A-moon_B-moon_opposition', 'synastry-moon-opposition-moon', 'relationship-moon-opposition-moon']::text[], '{"contentType":"synastry-kb-seed","source":"packages/astro-knowledge","sourcePath":"packages/astro-knowledge/data/synastry/aspects/A-moon_B-moon_opposition.json","collection":"synastry/aspects","kbId":"A-moon_B-moon_opposition","importedStatus":"REVIEWED"}'::jsonb, 'synastry-kb-v1', null, 'Moon Opposition Moon', null, 'You may both want to feel safe, understood, and supported, but reach those feelings in completely different ways. One of you may want to talk things through immediately, while the other needs time before they know what they feel. One of you reaches for closeness when upset, while the other needs space before they can reconnect.

This can create a frustrating dynamic where both people are trying to care for each other, yet neither feels fully cared for. The problem is rarely a lack of love or effort. It is that each person instinctively offers the kind of comfort they would want themselves.

When this works, it becomes a lesson in emotional flexibility. You begin to see that support can take many forms. Someone needing space is not necessarily pulling away from you. Someone needing reassurance is not necessarily asking for too much. The relationship stretches both people beyond their usual habits and teaches them new ways of giving and receiving care.

The real challenge is assuming that your way is the right way. When your emotional needs become the standard, the other person can start to feel unseen, even when your intentions are good. Over time, both people may end up feeling misunderstood by someone who genuinely cares.

The opportunity here is learning to get curious instead of making assumptions. Ask what helps. Pay attention to what actually settles the other person rather than what would settle you. The more you learn each other''s emotional language, the less time you spend missing each other and the more time you spend feeling truly understood.', '{"sections":[{"heading":"Deep meaning","body":"You may both want to feel safe, understood, and supported, but reach those feelings in completely different ways. One of you may want to talk things through immediately, while the other needs time before they know what they feel. One of you reaches for closeness when upset, while the other needs space before they can reconnect.\n\nThis can create a frustrating dynamic where both people are trying to care for each other, yet neither feels fully cared for. The problem is rarely a lack of love or effort. It is that each person instinctively offers the kind of comfort they would want themselves.\n\nWhen this works, it becomes a lesson in emotional flexibility. You begin to see that support can take many forms. Someone needing space is not necessarily pulling away from you. Someone needing reassurance is not necessarily asking for too much. The relationship stretches both people beyond their usual habits and teaches them new ways of giving and receiving care.\n\nThe real challenge is assuming that your way is the right way. When your emotional needs become the standard, the other person can start to feel unseen, even when your intentions are good. Over time, both people may end up feeling misunderstood by someone who genuinely cares.\n\nThe opportunity here is learning to get curious instead of making assumptions. Ask what helps. Pay attention to what actually settles the other person rather than what would settle you. The more you learn each other''s emotional language, the less time you spend missing each other and the more time you spend feeling truly understood."},{"heading":"Tension","body":"The real challenge is assuming that your way is the right way. When your emotional needs become the standard, the other person can start to feel unseen, even when your intentions are good. Over time, both people may end up feeling misunderstood by someone who genuinely cares."},{"heading":"Advice","body":"The opportunity here is learning to get curious instead of making assumptions. Ask what helps. Pay attention to what actually settles the other person rather than what would settle you. The more you learn each other''s emotional language, the less time you spend missing each other and the more time you spend feeling truly understood."}],"synastryKb":{"id":"A-moon_B-moon_opposition","kind":"interaspect","plainTranslation":"Complementary emotional opposites. One needs space where the other needs closeness; one processes aloud where the other goes quiet. Failure mode: each reading the other''s style as rejection. Name your needs explicitly - the contrast can balance you once you stop taking it personally.","summaryDeep":"You may both want to feel safe, understood, and supported, but reach those feelings in completely different ways. One of you may want to talk things through immediately, while the other needs time before they know what they feel. One of you reaches for closeness when upset, while the other needs space before they can reconnect.\n\nThis can create a frustrating dynamic where both people are trying to care for each other, yet neither feels fully cared for. The problem is rarely a lack of love or effort. It is that each person instinctively offers the kind of comfort they would want themselves.\n\nWhen this works, it becomes a lesson in emotional flexibility. You begin to see that support can take many forms. Someone needing space is not necessarily pulling away from you. Someone needing reassurance is not necessarily asking for too much. The relationship stretches both people beyond their usual habits and teaches them new ways of giving and receiving care.\n\nThe real challenge is assuming that your way is the right way. When your emotional needs become the standard, the other person can start to feel unseen, even when your intentions are good. Over time, both people may end up feeling misunderstood by someone who genuinely cares.\n\nThe opportunity here is learning to get curious instead of making assumptions. Ask what helps. Pay attention to what actually settles the other person rather than what would settle you. The more you learn each other''s emotional language, the less time you spend missing each other and the more time you spend feeling truly understood.","tension":"The real challenge is assuming that your way is the right way. When your emotional needs become the standard, the other person can start to feel unseen, even when your intentions are good. Over time, both people may end up feeling misunderstood by someone who genuinely cares.","advice":"The opportunity here is learning to get curious instead of making assumptions. Ask what helps. Pay attention to what actually settles the other person rather than what would settle you. The more you learn each other''s emotional language, the less time you spend missing each other and the more time you spend feeling truly understood.","policy":"Governed by synastry-policy. Descriptive, not deterministic; intensity is never treated as obligation, safety concerns override astrology.","note":"Imported from approved project marquee synastry aspect material. Source file was not copied into the repository.","status":"REVIEWED","authoringStatus":"locked","weight":2}}'::jsonb, 'synastry_aspect', 'Seeded from authored synastry KB. summaryShort is preserved as NULL when absent or null.', now()),
  ('A-moon_B-moon_sextile', 'synastry', 'in_depth', 'LIVE', 'synastry-aspect', null::date, '{"kind":"interaspect","planetA":"moon","planetB":"moon","aspect":"sextile"}'::jsonb, ARRAY['A-moon_B-moon_sextile', 'synastry-moon-sextile-moon', 'relationship-moon-sextile-moon']::text[], '{"contentType":"synastry-kb-seed","source":"packages/astro-knowledge","sourcePath":"packages/astro-knowledge/data/synastry/aspects/A-moon_B-moon_sextile.json","collection":"synastry/aspects","kbId":"A-moon_B-moon_sextile","importedStatus":"REVIEWED"}'::jsonb, 'synastry-kb-v1', null, 'Moon Sextile Moon', null, 'There is a natural emotional understanding here, but it tends to grow when both people make an effort. You may already have a sense of what the other person is feeling, but the connection gets stronger when you act on it.

When this works, the little things go a long way. Checking in when someone seems off. Remembering what helps when they are stressed. Giving them space when they need it, or being there when they want company. Small acts of care can make the relationship feel much safer over time.

What makes this connection valuable is that it teaches trust through experience rather than intensity. You may not have dramatic emotional breakthroughs together. Instead, you learn that this person notices you. They remember. They respond. Over time, that can create a deeper sense of security than grand declarations ever could.

This connection often asks both people to move from empathy into action. It is one thing to understand what someone is feeling. It is another to let that understanding shape how you treat them. The more you do that, the more the relationship becomes a place where both people feel emotionally supported rather than simply emotionally understood.

The challenge is that emotional understanding can create a false sense that nothing needs to be said. Because you often pick up on each other so easily, you may assume the other person already knows they are loved, appreciated, or important. Over time, that assumption can quietly replace expression. The connection still feels good, but it may stop deepening because neither person is putting words or actions around what they feel.

Being understood is not the same as feeling valued. People still need evidence of care, even when the bond feels obvious. This connection grows when you turn quiet understanding into something visible and consistent.', '{"sections":[{"heading":"Deep meaning","body":"There is a natural emotional understanding here, but it tends to grow when both people make an effort. You may already have a sense of what the other person is feeling, but the connection gets stronger when you act on it.\n\nWhen this works, the little things go a long way. Checking in when someone seems off. Remembering what helps when they are stressed. Giving them space when they need it, or being there when they want company. Small acts of care can make the relationship feel much safer over time.\n\nWhat makes this connection valuable is that it teaches trust through experience rather than intensity. You may not have dramatic emotional breakthroughs together. Instead, you learn that this person notices you. They remember. They respond. Over time, that can create a deeper sense of security than grand declarations ever could.\n\nThis connection often asks both people to move from empathy into action. It is one thing to understand what someone is feeling. It is another to let that understanding shape how you treat them. The more you do that, the more the relationship becomes a place where both people feel emotionally supported rather than simply emotionally understood.\n\nThe challenge is that emotional understanding can create a false sense that nothing needs to be said. Because you often pick up on each other so easily, you may assume the other person already knows they are loved, appreciated, or important. Over time, that assumption can quietly replace expression. The connection still feels good, but it may stop deepening because neither person is putting words or actions around what they feel.\n\nBeing understood is not the same as feeling valued. People still need evidence of care, even when the bond feels obvious. This connection grows when you turn quiet understanding into something visible and consistent."},{"heading":"Tension","body":"The challenge is that emotional understanding can create a false sense that nothing needs to be said. Because you often pick up on each other so easily, you may assume the other person already knows they are loved, appreciated, or important. Over time, that assumption can quietly replace expression. The connection still feels good, but it may stop deepening because neither person is putting words or actions around what they feel."},{"heading":"Advice","body":"Being understood is not the same as feeling valued. People still need evidence of care, even when the bond feels obvious. This connection grows when you turn quiet understanding into something visible and consistent."}],"synastryKb":{"id":"A-moon_B-moon_sextile","kind":"interaspect","plainTranslation":"Easy emotional understanding with a little complementary difference. You read each other well and your needs mesh without friction. Failure mode: assuming you''re identical and missing where you differ. Use the rapport, stay curious about the gaps.","summaryDeep":"There is a natural emotional understanding here, but it tends to grow when both people make an effort. You may already have a sense of what the other person is feeling, but the connection gets stronger when you act on it.\n\nWhen this works, the little things go a long way. Checking in when someone seems off. Remembering what helps when they are stressed. Giving them space when they need it, or being there when they want company. Small acts of care can make the relationship feel much safer over time.\n\nWhat makes this connection valuable is that it teaches trust through experience rather than intensity. You may not have dramatic emotional breakthroughs together. Instead, you learn that this person notices you. They remember. They respond. Over time, that can create a deeper sense of security than grand declarations ever could.\n\nThis connection often asks both people to move from empathy into action. It is one thing to understand what someone is feeling. It is another to let that understanding shape how you treat them. The more you do that, the more the relationship becomes a place where both people feel emotionally supported rather than simply emotionally understood.\n\nThe challenge is that emotional understanding can create a false sense that nothing needs to be said. Because you often pick up on each other so easily, you may assume the other person already knows they are loved, appreciated, or important. Over time, that assumption can quietly replace expression. The connection still feels good, but it may stop deepening because neither person is putting words or actions around what they feel.\n\nBeing understood is not the same as feeling valued. People still need evidence of care, even when the bond feels obvious. This connection grows when you turn quiet understanding into something visible and consistent.","tension":"The challenge is that emotional understanding can create a false sense that nothing needs to be said. Because you often pick up on each other so easily, you may assume the other person already knows they are loved, appreciated, or important. Over time, that assumption can quietly replace expression. The connection still feels good, but it may stop deepening because neither person is putting words or actions around what they feel.","advice":"Being understood is not the same as feeling valued. People still need evidence of care, even when the bond feels obvious. This connection grows when you turn quiet understanding into something visible and consistent.","policy":"Governed by synastry-policy. Descriptive, not deterministic; intensity is never treated as obligation, safety concerns override astrology.","note":"Imported from approved project marquee synastry aspect material. Source file was not copied into the repository.","status":"REVIEWED","authoringStatus":"locked","weight":2}}'::jsonb, 'synastry_aspect', 'Seeded from authored synastry KB. summaryShort is preserved as NULL when absent or null.', now()),
  ('A-moon_B-moon_square', 'synastry', 'in_depth', 'LIVE', 'synastry-aspect', null::date, '{"kind":"interaspect","planetA":"moon","planetB":"moon","aspect":"square"}'::jsonb, ARRAY['A-moon_B-moon_square', 'synastry-moon-square-moon', 'relationship-moon-square-moon']::text[], '{"contentType":"synastry-kb-seed","source":"packages/astro-knowledge","sourcePath":"packages/astro-knowledge/data/synastry/aspects/A-moon_B-moon_square.json","collection":"synastry/aspects","kbId":"A-moon_B-moon_square","importedStatus":"REVIEWED"}'::jsonb, 'synastry-kb-v1', null, 'Moon Square Moon', null, 'This person may unsettle you in small ways that are hard to explain. It might be how they handle silence, how quickly they need reassurance, how they pull back when something is wrong, or how they expect you to respond when they are upset. Their emotional style can press on yours.

When this works, it can make you more honest about your own patterns. You may start to notice what you do when you feel hurt, ignored, overwhelmed, or unsure. This person may not always make you comfortable, but they can show you where you get reactive, where you shut down, or where you expect someone else to know what you have not said.

The lesson is learning the difference between what happened and what it touched in you. Sometimes the other person really did miss you. Sometimes they only moved differently than you would have. Sometimes their mood is not about you, even if your body reacts like it is.

The harder part is that moods can start setting each other off. One person gets quiet and the other reads it as rejection. One person asks for closeness and the other feels crowded. One person tries to move on and the other feels dismissed. Suddenly the issue is not just what happened, but how both people felt about how it was handled.

Because this is emotional, it may not always make sense on paper. You can explain the facts perfectly and still feel bruised. That is what makes this contact tricky. The feelings are not always logical, but they are still real.

Pause before you turn the mood into a story. Ask what is actually needed. This connection gets easier when both people stop treating sensitivity as an accusation.', '{"sections":[{"heading":"Deep meaning","body":"This person may unsettle you in small ways that are hard to explain. It might be how they handle silence, how quickly they need reassurance, how they pull back when something is wrong, or how they expect you to respond when they are upset. Their emotional style can press on yours.\n\nWhen this works, it can make you more honest about your own patterns. You may start to notice what you do when you feel hurt, ignored, overwhelmed, or unsure. This person may not always make you comfortable, but they can show you where you get reactive, where you shut down, or where you expect someone else to know what you have not said.\n\nThe lesson is learning the difference between what happened and what it touched in you. Sometimes the other person really did miss you. Sometimes they only moved differently than you would have. Sometimes their mood is not about you, even if your body reacts like it is.\n\nThe harder part is that moods can start setting each other off. One person gets quiet and the other reads it as rejection. One person asks for closeness and the other feels crowded. One person tries to move on and the other feels dismissed. Suddenly the issue is not just what happened, but how both people felt about how it was handled.\n\nBecause this is emotional, it may not always make sense on paper. You can explain the facts perfectly and still feel bruised. That is what makes this contact tricky. The feelings are not always logical, but they are still real.\n\nPause before you turn the mood into a story. Ask what is actually needed. This connection gets easier when both people stop treating sensitivity as an accusation."},{"heading":"Tension","body":"The harder part is that moods can start setting each other off. One person gets quiet and the other reads it as rejection. One person asks for closeness and the other feels crowded. One person tries to move on and the other feels dismissed. Suddenly the issue is not just what happened, but how both people felt about how it was handled.\n\nBecause this is emotional, it may not always make sense on paper. You can explain the facts perfectly and still feel bruised. That is what makes this contact tricky. The feelings are not always logical, but they are still real."},{"heading":"Advice","body":"Pause before you turn the mood into a story. Ask what is actually needed. This connection gets easier when both people stop treating sensitivity as an accusation."}],"synastryKb":{"id":"A-moon_B-moon_square","kind":"interaspect","plainTranslation":"Your emotional needs clash at the root. You both feel strongly but in incompatible ways - what soothes one unsettles the other. Failure mode: two reactive people triggering each other with no neutral ground. Learn each other''s actual needs instead of assuming yours apply; this one takes translation work.","summaryDeep":"This person may unsettle you in small ways that are hard to explain. It might be how they handle silence, how quickly they need reassurance, how they pull back when something is wrong, or how they expect you to respond when they are upset. Their emotional style can press on yours.\n\nWhen this works, it can make you more honest about your own patterns. You may start to notice what you do when you feel hurt, ignored, overwhelmed, or unsure. This person may not always make you comfortable, but they can show you where you get reactive, where you shut down, or where you expect someone else to know what you have not said.\n\nThe lesson is learning the difference between what happened and what it touched in you. Sometimes the other person really did miss you. Sometimes they only moved differently than you would have. Sometimes their mood is not about you, even if your body reacts like it is.\n\nThe harder part is that moods can start setting each other off. One person gets quiet and the other reads it as rejection. One person asks for closeness and the other feels crowded. One person tries to move on and the other feels dismissed. Suddenly the issue is not just what happened, but how both people felt about how it was handled.\n\nBecause this is emotional, it may not always make sense on paper. You can explain the facts perfectly and still feel bruised. That is what makes this contact tricky. The feelings are not always logical, but they are still real.\n\nPause before you turn the mood into a story. Ask what is actually needed. This connection gets easier when both people stop treating sensitivity as an accusation.","tension":"The harder part is that moods can start setting each other off. One person gets quiet and the other reads it as rejection. One person asks for closeness and the other feels crowded. One person tries to move on and the other feels dismissed. Suddenly the issue is not just what happened, but how both people felt about how it was handled.\n\nBecause this is emotional, it may not always make sense on paper. You can explain the facts perfectly and still feel bruised. That is what makes this contact tricky. The feelings are not always logical, but they are still real.","advice":"Pause before you turn the mood into a story. Ask what is actually needed. This connection gets easier when both people stop treating sensitivity as an accusation.","policy":"Governed by synastry-policy. Descriptive, not deterministic; intensity is never treated as obligation, safety concerns override astrology.","note":"Imported from approved project marquee synastry aspect material. Source file was not copied into the repository.","status":"REVIEWED","authoringStatus":"locked","weight":2}}'::jsonb, 'synastry_aspect', 'Seeded from authored synastry KB. summaryShort is preserved as NULL when absent or null.', now()),
  ('A-moon_B-moon_trine', 'synastry', 'in_depth', 'LIVE', 'synastry-aspect', null::date, '{"kind":"interaspect","planetA":"moon","planetB":"moon","aspect":"trine"}'::jsonb, ARRAY['A-moon_B-moon_trine', 'synastry-moon-trine-moon', 'relationship-moon-trine-moon']::text[], '{"contentType":"synastry-kb-seed","source":"packages/astro-knowledge","sourcePath":"packages/astro-knowledge/data/synastry/aspects/A-moon_B-moon_trine.json","collection":"synastry/aspects","kbId":"A-moon_B-moon_trine","importedStatus":"REVIEWED"}'::jsonb, 'synastry-kb-v1', null, 'Moon Trine Moon', null, 'Being around this person may feel like a relief. You do not have to monitor yourself as closely, explain every feeling, or worry that a mood will become a problem. You can be quiet without it feeling awkward, emotional without it feeling overwhelming, or simply not at your best without feeling like you need to hide it.

When this works, there is a deep sense of emotional permission between you. This person may understand what you need without making you spell it out. They know when to come closer, when to give space, and when to simply let you be. Around them, you may feel less judged, less guarded, and more able to relax into who you are.

What makes this connection special is that it often lowers the amount of emotional work both people are doing without realizing it. You may notice that you spend less time defending your feelings, second-guessing your reactions, or wondering whether you are too much or not enough. Instead of constantly managing the relationship, you can put more energy into actually living your life together.

This kind of ease can also be quietly healing. When someone consistently responds to your feelings in ways that make sense to you, it becomes easier to trust your own emotional experience. You may become less reactive, less guarded, and less afraid of being misunderstood. The relationship does not just feel safe. It can help both people become safer within themselves.

The growth in this connection comes from recognizing that comfort is not the same thing as growth. Because you understand each other so naturally, it can be easy to stay with what is familiar and assume that is enough. But the real value of this connection is that it gives both people room to keep changing, keep learning about each other, and keep showing sides of themselves that have not been seen yet.

The challenge is subtle because nothing feels obviously wrong. The emotional fit can be so effortless that you stop noticing it. What once felt precious becomes familiar. What felt healing becomes expected.

Do not overlook what makes this connection work. Protect the softness. Keep showing up for each other in small ways. The ease between you is a gift, and it becomes even stronger when both people actively nurture it instead of assuming it will always take care of itself.', '{"sections":[{"heading":"Deep meaning","body":"Being around this person may feel like a relief. You do not have to monitor yourself as closely, explain every feeling, or worry that a mood will become a problem. You can be quiet without it feeling awkward, emotional without it feeling overwhelming, or simply not at your best without feeling like you need to hide it.\n\nWhen this works, there is a deep sense of emotional permission between you. This person may understand what you need without making you spell it out. They know when to come closer, when to give space, and when to simply let you be. Around them, you may feel less judged, less guarded, and more able to relax into who you are.\n\nWhat makes this connection special is that it often lowers the amount of emotional work both people are doing without realizing it. You may notice that you spend less time defending your feelings, second-guessing your reactions, or wondering whether you are too much or not enough. Instead of constantly managing the relationship, you can put more energy into actually living your life together.\n\nThis kind of ease can also be quietly healing. When someone consistently responds to your feelings in ways that make sense to you, it becomes easier to trust your own emotional experience. You may become less reactive, less guarded, and less afraid of being misunderstood. The relationship does not just feel safe. It can help both people become safer within themselves.\n\nThe growth in this connection comes from recognizing that comfort is not the same thing as growth. Because you understand each other so naturally, it can be easy to stay with what is familiar and assume that is enough. But the real value of this connection is that it gives both people room to keep changing, keep learning about each other, and keep showing sides of themselves that have not been seen yet.\n\nThe challenge is subtle because nothing feels obviously wrong. The emotional fit can be so effortless that you stop noticing it. What once felt precious becomes familiar. What felt healing becomes expected.\n\nDo not overlook what makes this connection work. Protect the softness. Keep showing up for each other in small ways. The ease between you is a gift, and it becomes even stronger when both people actively nurture it instead of assuming it will always take care of itself."},{"heading":"Tension","body":"The growth in this connection comes from recognizing that comfort is not the same thing as growth. Because you understand each other so naturally, it can be easy to stay with what is familiar and assume that is enough. But the real value of this connection is that it gives both people room to keep changing, keep learning about each other, and keep showing sides of themselves that have not been seen yet.\n\nThe challenge is subtle because nothing feels obviously wrong. The emotional fit can be so effortless that you stop noticing it. What once felt precious becomes familiar. What felt healing becomes expected."},{"heading":"Advice","body":"Do not overlook what makes this connection work. Protect the softness. Keep showing up for each other in small ways. The ease between you is a gift, and it becomes even stronger when both people actively nurture it instead of assuming it will always take care of itself."}],"synastryKb":{"id":"A-moon_B-moon_trine","kind":"interaspect","plainTranslation":"Feelings flow between you effortlessly. Deep comfort, intuitive understanding, a relationship that soothes. Failure mode: so comfortable it can lack edge or growth. Enjoy the ease; bring in some challenge so it doesn''t go soft.","summaryDeep":"Being around this person may feel like a relief. You do not have to monitor yourself as closely, explain every feeling, or worry that a mood will become a problem. You can be quiet without it feeling awkward, emotional without it feeling overwhelming, or simply not at your best without feeling like you need to hide it.\n\nWhen this works, there is a deep sense of emotional permission between you. This person may understand what you need without making you spell it out. They know when to come closer, when to give space, and when to simply let you be. Around them, you may feel less judged, less guarded, and more able to relax into who you are.\n\nWhat makes this connection special is that it often lowers the amount of emotional work both people are doing without realizing it. You may notice that you spend less time defending your feelings, second-guessing your reactions, or wondering whether you are too much or not enough. Instead of constantly managing the relationship, you can put more energy into actually living your life together.\n\nThis kind of ease can also be quietly healing. When someone consistently responds to your feelings in ways that make sense to you, it becomes easier to trust your own emotional experience. You may become less reactive, less guarded, and less afraid of being misunderstood. The relationship does not just feel safe. It can help both people become safer within themselves.\n\nThe growth in this connection comes from recognizing that comfort is not the same thing as growth. Because you understand each other so naturally, it can be easy to stay with what is familiar and assume that is enough. But the real value of this connection is that it gives both people room to keep changing, keep learning about each other, and keep showing sides of themselves that have not been seen yet.\n\nThe challenge is subtle because nothing feels obviously wrong. The emotional fit can be so effortless that you stop noticing it. What once felt precious becomes familiar. What felt healing becomes expected.\n\nDo not overlook what makes this conne